Dockplane agent
===============

A statically linked Linux binary and the systemd unit it is meant to run
under. On Debian and Ubuntu, prefer the .deb: it creates the service account,
installs the unit in the right place and preserves your identity across
upgrades. This tarball is the manual route for everything else.

Contents
--------

  dockplane-agent           the binary
  dockplane-agent.service   the systemd unit
  agent.env                 optional settings, referenced by the unit
  README                    this file

Nothing here is host-specific. There is no identity, no certificate, no
enrollment token and no server address: the agent is given those once, when it
enrolls.

Installing
----------

  sudo install -m 0755 dockplane-agent /usr/bin/dockplane-agent
  sudo install -m 0644 dockplane-agent.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/
  sudo install -d -m 0750 /etc/dockplane-agent
  sudo install -m 0640 agent.env /etc/dockplane-agent/agent.env

The unit expects the binary at /usr/bin/dockplane-agent. Create the account it
runs as:

  sudo groupadd --system dockplane-agent
  sudo useradd --system --gid dockplane-agent --no-create-home \
    --home-dir /nonexistent --shell /usr/sbin/nologin dockplane-agent

  sudo systemctl daemon-reload

Enrolling
---------

  1. Create an enrollment token in Dockplane. It is single-use and short-lived.

  2. sudo -u dockplane-agent \
       DOCKPLANE_AGENT_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/dockplane-agent \
       dockplane-agent enroll --server https://dockplane.example.com --token-stdin

     There is no --token flag on purpose: a token on a command line is visible
     to every user on the machine and is kept in the shell history.

  3. sudo systemctl enable --now dockplane-agent

Docker socket access
--------------------

The unit gives the service account membership of the `docker` group.

**That is equivalent to root on this host.** Anything able to talk to the
Docker daemon can start a privileged container that mounts the filesystem. It
is unavoidable for managing Docker, and it is why the agent has no way to run
a command: its operations are fixed at build time, and the three that change
anything take a container identifier and nothing else.

Files
-----

  /usr/bin/dockplane-agent                        the binary
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/dockplane-agent.service the unit
  /etc/dockplane-agent/agent.env                  optional settings
  /var/lib/dockplane-agent/                       identity and private key, 0700

The state directory is created by systemd on first start. Back it up if you
would rather not enroll the host again; whoever has it can act as this host.

Verify what you downloaded
--------------------------

  sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS

Removing
--------

Stop and disable the service, delete the files above. Deleting
/var/lib/dockplane-agent destroys this host's identity: revoke the agent in
Dockplane as well, because its certificate stays valid until you do.
